{"id":"NCT07174414","title":"Cilostazol for Prevention of Recurrent Stroke Trial","official":"Cilostazol for Prevention of Recurrent Stroke Trial","summary":"The goal of this clinical study is to learn if adding a medicine called cilostazol to usual treatment can help prevent another stroke, a heart attack, or death in patients who had a stroke caused by a blocked blood vessel, or a \"mini-stroke\" (also called a TIA), in the past 6 months.\n\nTo determine if cilostazol is safe and effective at lowering the chance of strokes, heart attacks and deaths, the study will compare cilostazol to a placebo, a pill that looks the same as cilostazol but has no medicine in it.\n\nParticipants will:\n\n* Be assigned by chance to take either cilostazol or a placebo, along with their usual medicine.\n* Not know which one they are taking, and neither will the study team, which keeps the results fair.\n* Be followed for up to 5 years to see if they have a stroke, heart attack, or major bleeding.","conditions":["Stroke Recurrence","Myocardial Infarction","Vascular Death","Ischemic Stroke","TIA (Transient Ischemic Attack)","Stroke (CVA) or Transient Ischemic Attack","Recurrent Stroke"],"interventions":[{"type":"DRUG","name":"Cilostazol 100 mg Tablets"},{"type":"DRUG","name":"Placebo"}],"criteria":"Inclusion Criteria:\n\n1. ≥40 years\n2. Ischemic stroke or high-risk TIA (ABCD2 score ≥6)\n3. Can be randomized within 180 days after ischemic stroke or high-risk TIA\n4. Treatment with single or dual antithrombotic therapy with aspirin, clopidogrel and/or ticagrelor per international and/or national guidelines\n5. Two or more of the following risk factors: age 65 years or older, hypertension, diabetes mellitus, history of ischemic stroke other than the qualifying event for this trial, currently smoking, peripheral arterial disease, coronary artery disease including prior MI, aortic atherosclerotic plaque ≥4 mm, ≥50% stenosis or occlusion of a major intracranial artery (ICA, M1, M2, A1, P1, vertebral, or basilar) or extracranial artery (ICA or vertebral)\n6. In the opinion of the site investigator, patient is medically stable, capable of participating in the trial, and willing and able to attend follow-up\n7. Able to receive study drug by mail and able to begin taking study drug promptly after receipt\n8. Provision of signed and dated informed consent form by patient or legally authorized representative\n\nExclusion Criteria:\n\n1. Atrial fibrillation, mechanical heart valve, or left ventricular assist device\n2. Use of prohibited medication (as specified in the MOP)\n3. Modified Rankin Scale 5\n4. Planned cardiac surgery, percutaneous coronary intervention, carotid endarterectomy or stenting (patients are eligible post-procedure)\n5. History of symptomatic non-traumatic intracerebral hemorrhage within last 2 years\n6. Known thrombocytopenia (platelet count ≤100,000 per microliter (µL))\n7. Severe liver dysfunction (active hepatitis or hepatic insufficiency with Child-Pugh score B or C)\n8. Pregnant, breast-feeding, or of child-bearing potential but not on birth control\n9. Confirmed diagnosis of heart failure with current or prior symptoms or signs (e.g. paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, reduced exercise tolerance, ankle swelling, elevated jugular venous pressure)\n10. Hypersensitivity to cilostazol or any component of the formulation (e.g. anaphylaxis, angioedema)\n11. Currently enrolled in an investigational intervention study\n12. Life expectancy less than 6 months\n13. Participant does not allow contacting of their surrogate (e.g. family member or friend) or health care practitioner to obtain health information should participant become unwilling or unable to follow the study schedule.\n14. Participants who are incarcerated, including prisoners or participants compulsorily detained for treatment of psychiatric disease.","minAge":"40 Years","maxAge":"","sex":"ALL","healthy":false,"studyType":"INTERVENTIONAL","purpose":"PREVENTION","enrollment":2000,"st